  "answer": "I'll start by inspecting the workspace.\nThe bug is clear: `add` subtracts instead of adding. Let me confirm the failing baseline first.\nBaseline confirmed failing (`add(2,3)` → `-1`). Now the minimal fix:\nFixed. `bun test` passes: **1 pass, 0 fail, 5 expect() calls**.\n\n## What was wrong\n`src/add.ts` implemented subtraction inside the adder:\n\n```ts\nexport function add(a: number, b: number) { return a - b }   // bug\n```\n\nThe test caught it immediately on the first case: `add(2, 3)` returned `-1`, expected `5`.\n\n## The fix\nA single-character operator change — the body now adds:\n\n```ts\nexport function add(a: number, b: number) { return a + b }\n```\n\n## Constraints respected\n- **Public signature unchanged**: still `export function add(a: number, b: number)` — same name, parameter types, arity, and return type (inferred `number`).\n- **Tests untouched**: `test/oracle.test.ts` was only read, never modified.\n- **No config or runner changes**: `package.json` and `AGENTS.md` untouched; `bun:test` remains the runner, no new files added.\n- Only `src/add.ts` changed (SHA-256 `6120818a…` → `3436e9fa…`).\n\nThe single `+`/`-` operator swap is sufficient because the whole signed-input matrix in the oracle (positive, mixed-sign, zero, both-negative, carry across a power of ten) is exercised by the same code path — no sign-handling branch exists to fix separately."
